==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|parasite|ism}}. Cognate with {{cog|fr|parasitisme}} and {{cog|pt|-}} and {{cog|es|parasitismo}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpæɹəs(a)ɪˌtɪzm̩/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|par|a|sit|ism}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|ecology}} Interaction between two organisms, in which one organism (the [[parasite]]) benefits and the other (the [[host]]) is harmed.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A similar interaction between people.
#: ''We accused her of '''parasitism''' in taking his hard-earned money for new dresses.''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1674|chapter=Observations upon the Articles of Peace with the Iriſh Rebels|title=The Works of John Milton, Hiſtorical, Political, and Miſcellaneous|volume=1|location=London|publisher=A. Millar|year_published=1753|page=399|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=EhwcAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA399|passage=Either theſe men imagine themſelves to be marvelouſly high ſet and exalted in the chair of Belfaſt, to vouchſafe the parliament of England no better ſtile than Servants, or elſe their high notion, which we rather believe, falls as low as court '''paraſitiſm''' ; ſuppoſing all men to be ſervants, but the king.}}

